None of the other libraries the wheel's files need at run time is carried: not
the C++ runtime the Mojo runtime libraries need (libstdc++.so.6, libgcc_s.so.1),
not the system's glibc (`libc.so.6`, `libpthread.so.0`), and not the codec
libraries libz, liblz4, libzstd, libbz2 and liblzma, which komira opens by name
from the host when a file uses that codec. The macOS arm64 wheel carries no
owner library: its libkomira.dylib links the C archives of its closure (the
four libraries above among them) statically, so whatever of that third-party
code it calls is inside libkomira.dylib, under the same notices.

The Mojo runtime: Modular's libraries, carried in the bundled wheel
-------------------------------------------------------------------

komira's libraries, the shared library libkomira among them, are compiled
by Modular's Mojo compiler and need the Mojo runtime libraries when they are
loaded (libKGENCompilerRTShared, libAsyncRTRuntimeGlobals and
libMSupportGlobals; .so on Linux, .dylib on macOS). These libraries are
Modular's, are not part of this repository, and are not covered by its
Apache License grant.

The Python wheel `komira` is built in two flavours (python/flavour.bzl), and a
release publishes one of them.

The bundled flavour carries these three files, unmodified, at
`komira/_lib/lib/`: libKGENCompilerRTShared.so, libAsyncRTRuntimeGlobals.so
and libMSupportGlobals.so, taken from the Mojo compiler package that compiled
libkomira (Modular's conda package `mojo-compiler` 1.1.0, pinned by sha256 in
tools/build/platforms/table.bzl). It carries no other file of Modular's.

The external flavour carries none of them and no other file of Modular's. It
declares a dependency on Modular's PyPI package `mojo-compiler` at the
compiler version libkomira was built with, which the user's package installer
fetches from PyPI under the licence that package declares, and it loads the
three libraries from where that package installs them. Its
License-Expression is `Apache-2.0`, and it carries LICENSE and this file
only. The paragraph below is about the bundled flavour.

The licence those files are distributed under is the one their package
states. That package's metadata declares `LicenseRef-Modular-Proprietary`,
and its licence file, `info/licenses/LICENSE`, is the Modular Community
License Terms; its `info/licenses/Third-Party-Notices` lists the third-party
components of Modular's software. Neither file, nor the three libraries,
states the Apache License: Modular's statement that Mojo is licensed under
the Apache License v2.0 with LLVM Exceptions is made for the source code it
publishes, and the package of these binary files does not repeat it. The
bundled wheel carries both files, unmodified, as `.dist-info/licenses/MODULAR_LICENSE`
and `.dist-info/licenses/MODULAR_THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES`, and its
License-Expression is `Apache-2.0 AND LicenseRef-Modular-Proprietary`: komira's
own files under the first, the three Mojo runtime libraries under the second.
docs/design/mojo_runtime_pypi_vs_conda.md records where each statement was
read.

komira's conda packages carry none of these libraries. Each declares a
dependency on Modular's `mojo-compiler` conda package at the compiler version
the libraries were built with, and the user's package installer fetches that
package from Modular's channel under the licence that package declares. A
conda package carries only LICENSE.

`mojo_bundle` outputs (tools/build/package), such as the base image under
packaging/images, also carry the Mojo runtime libraries in their `lib/`.
